    "result_text": "The build is green. The full test suite (the single `AcceptanceTest`, whose `coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity` case is the new behaviour) passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` flag: it returns `true` when the owner's city already holds **40\u201349** owners at creation time (approaching the capacity limit of 50). The hard 409 rejection at 50 in `RequireCityCapacity` is untouched.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`AssignCapacityWarning.java`** (new) \u2014 a pipeline step that counts existing owners in the new owner's city (using the same case-insensitive/whitespace-collapsed normalization as `RequireCityCapacity`) and sets `capacityWarning = count >= 40 && count < 50`. It runs before `SaveOwner`, so the owner being created isn't counted against itself \u2014 mirroring the existing `AssignBulkSignupWarning` pattern.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step into the create pipeline (after `assignBulkWarning`, before `assignPossibleDuplicate`) under `govern: [ transaction ]`.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `capacityWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct maps it by name into `OwnerDto`.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `capacityWarning` boolean property to the owner schema so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it in the response.\n\n5. **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `postgres`, `mysql`) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning` column as the last owner column, and appended the corresponding value to the positional seed inserts in the hsqldb `data.sql` (the only file that inserts owners positionally).",
